```dogwood
@id("allow_readonly_lookups")
permit (
  principal,
  action in [
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder",
    Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"list_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes"
  ],
  resource
);

@id("close_workorder_main_only_after_confirmed_human_approval")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.decision: "APPROVED",
    output.approver_kind: "HUMAN"
  }
};
```

The schema doesn't say which strings `authority_get_approval` returns, so `"APPROVED"` and `"HUMAN"` are my guesses. Please check them against the real tool before deploying.

The close permit now relies only on a completed `authority_get_approval` response. The response must be for the same `wonum` and `site_id`, be less than an hour old, and carry an approved decision from a human approver.

`approve_workorder` has no permit, so the agent cannot approve anything itself. Every other mutating tool is also denied by default.

I did not tie the approver to the agent's own identity. `approver_id` is a plain string, so the policy can't compare it to the principal.
